1
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:12,744
I record myself checking one invoice from our staffing vendor.

2
00:00:13,062 --> 00:00:15,862
I'm checking this month's invoice from Halcyon Staffing.

3
00:00:18,285 --> 00:00:20,666
Every rate on it has to match our contract.

4
00:00:20,766 --> 00:00:23,146
And nobody should be billed twice for the same week.

5
00:00:23,246 --> 00:00:25,496
Then I check each person's hours against their timecard.

6
00:00:25,596 --> 00:00:28,396
Maya is approved for 40 hours. Billed hours can't go over that.

7
00:00:32,102 --> 00:00:34,899
Sam is approved for 36.

8
00:00:34,999 --> 00:00:37,799
If anything is off, I dispute it and ask for a credit.

9
00:00:40,111 --> 00:00:44,387
When I stop, each sentence shows up linked to the steps it describes.

10
00:00:44,487 --> 00:00:52,487
The recorder saves each click with the exact element it hit, and every sentence I say with its timing, linked to the clicks around it.

11
00:00:52,687 --> 00:00:56,187
The click shows where the evidence is; the sentence says the rule.

12
00:00:56,287 --> 00:01:00,220
I send it to Ramp.

13
00:01:00,320 --> 00:01:06,220
What I recorded becomes a workflow. When a new invoice arrives, an agent runs it in its own browser and brings back evidence.

14
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:11,357
Bill Pay learns the workflow, one stage at a time.

15
00:01:11,505 --> 00:01:15,645
Here is what it learned.

16
00:01:15,793 --> 00:01:19,091
Each rule points back to what I said and where I clicked.

17
00:01:19,461 --> 00:01:21,676
The recording keeps my words in time with my clicks.

18
00:01:21,776 --> 00:01:28,576
Claude reads the recording and writes the workflow: each rule with the words and click it came from, and where the proof lives.

19
00:01:28,776 --> 00:01:33,050
The checks are code we wrote; what it learned is what to check and where.

20
00:01:33,150 --> 00:01:39,752
It passed its evals, so v1 is now the active workflow.

21
00:01:40,152 --> 00:01:42,181
A new invoice from the same vendor arrives.

22
00:01:42,329 --> 00:01:46,158
Ramp picks the workflow and starts its agent.

23
00:01:46,258 --> 00:01:50,258
The agent's own browser runs right here in the bill.

24
00:01:50,406 --> 00:01:54,758
Sped up, it reads each timecard the way I did.

25
00:01:54,958 --> 00:02:01,985
The run is under Workflows, every model turn and tool call.

26
00:02:02,207 --> 00:02:08,148
Each step has its tokens, cost and request ID.

27
00:02:08,422 --> 00:02:10,845
Every fact was read again from its page by plain code.

28
00:02:10,993 --> 00:02:15,498
Three problems, $3448 to credit. I dispute it. The agent never approves or pays.

29
00:02:15,598 --> 00:02:21,998
The agent works in its own browser. Each turn, Claude sees the page as an outline, picks an action and gets the result.

30
00:02:22,198 --> 00:02:28,498
It records facts with the page they came from. Code turns them into pass or fail, Claude writes the receipt, and I decide.

31
00:02:31,788 --> 00:02:35,688
Next, a clean invoice from the same vendor.

32
00:02:35,888 --> 00:02:43,808
Everything checks out. One click from me, and it moves on to payment.

33
00:02:43,908 --> 00:02:49,708
We test the checks against past corrections, the learning against recordings with known answers, and the whole review end to end.

34
00:02:49,908 --> 00:02:55,808
One review was 13 Claude calls, 56 seconds and $0.22, and each call has a request ID we can look up.

35
00:02:55,908 --> 00:02:58,908
Today, one recording becomes a tested workflow with evidence behind every recommendation.

36
00:02:59,108 --> 00:03:02,308
Next, it could pull a delivery confirmation from Teams or Slack and attach it to the receipt.
